- How does varying batch size B affect latency and memory?
- How would you extend to handle sliding windows or duplicates efficiently?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests your ability to design a fixed-memory streaming algorithm for heavy hitter detection—a common requirement in real-world scenarios like analytics dashboards, network monitoring, and recommendation systems.

## Key Concepts
- **Misra-Gries algorithm**: A space-efficient algorithm for finding items that appear more than 1/m of the time
- **Heavy hitters**: Values that appear frequently in a stream (above a threshold)
- **Space-time tradeoffs**: Analyzing memory usage versus per-element update time

## Algorithm Analysis
| Metric | Complexity |
|--------|------------|
| Per-element update | O(1) |
| Per-batch query | O(m) = O(1) since m=2 |
| Memory usage | O(m) = O(2) = O(1) fixed |

## Code Example
```javascript
class MisraGries {
  constructor(m = 2) {
    this.m = m;
    this.counters = new Map();
  }

  add(x) {
    if (this.counters.has(x)) {
      this.counters.set(x, this.counters.get(x) + 1);
    } else if (this.counters.size < this.m) {
      this.counters.set(x, 1);
    } else {
      for (const [key, val] of this.counters) {
        this.counters.set(key, val - 1);
        if (this.counters.get(key) === 0) this.counters.delete(key);
      }
    }
  }

  getHeavyHitters() {
    return Array.from(this.counters.entries())
      .filter(([_, count]) => count > 0)
      .map(([value, count]) => ({ value, count }));
  }
}
```

## Small Example (m=2, stream: 1, 2, 1, 3, 1, 2)
| Element | Counter State | Heavy Hitters |
|---------|---------------|---------------|
| 1 | {1:1} | 1 |
| 2 | {1:1, 2:1} | 1, 2 |
| 1 | {1:2, 2:1} | 1, 2 |
| 3 | {1:1, 2:0} → {1:1} | 1 |
| 1 | {1:2} | 1 |
| 2 | {1:2, 2:1} | 1, 2 |

## Follow-up Questions
- **Sliding windows**: Use a timestamped variant or combine with a separate structure tracking element ages
- **Ties**: Resolve by recency (most recently incremented) or arbitrarily since counts are equal
- **Accuracy**: Misra-Gries guarantees all heavy hitters are found but may include false positives; lowering the threshold (increasing m) reduces false positives at higher memory cost

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to reason about incremental data structures and Bloom filter trade-offs for query pruning in streaming text.

## Key Concepts
- Incremental suffix automaton with amortized O(1) update per character
- Bloom filters for O(1) query pruning per pattern, per batch
- Cache behavior: larger B reduces SAM update frequency but increases Bloom filter staleness and memory pressure
- Space: each block's Bloom filter costs O(B) bits; false positives introduce extra SAM verifications

## Code Example
```javascript
// Extend SAM with a block of text
function extendSAM(sam, block) {
  for (const char of block) {
    sam.extend(char);
  }
}

// Build Bloom filter for a block's substrings
function buildBlockBloom(block, size) {
  const bloom = new BloomFilter(size, 0.01);
  for (let i = 0; i < block.length; i++) {
    for (let len = 1; len <= block.length - i; len++) {
      bloom.add(block.slice(i, i + len));
    }
  }
  return bloom;
}

// Query patterns against accumulated text
function queryPatterns(sam, bloomFilters, patterns) {
  return patterns.map(p => {
    const mightMatch = bloomFilters.every(bf => bf.test(p));
    return mightMatch && sam.contains(p);
  });
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests understanding of streaming algorithms for distinct counting with bounded memory, including practical trade-offs between accuracy and resources.

## Key Concepts
- **HyperLogLog sketches**: Probabilistic data structure using leading-zero counting to estimate cardinalities
- **Memory-accuracy trade-off**: With m registers, expected relative error is ~1.04/√m (e.g., 13% error with 64 registers)
- **Per-element update**: O(1) time - hash, extract bucket, compute rank, update register
- **Batch processing**: Process each element in the batch sequentially, output estimate after all updates
- **Comparison to exact counting**: Exact counting requires O(N) memory to store all seen values (or at minimum a bitmap of size M), while HLL uses constant O(m) memory regardless of N

## Code Example
```javascript
class HyperLogLog {
  constructor(m = 64) {
    this.m = m;
    this.registers = new Uint8Array(m);
  }

  update(x) {
    const h = this.hash64(x);
    const bucketBits = Math.log2(this.m);
    const bucket = h >> (64 - bucketBits);
    const rank = this.leadingZeros((h << bucketBits) | 1) + 1;
    this.registers[bucket] = Math.max(this.registers[bucket], rank);
  }

  estimate() {
    const sum = this.registers.reduce((acc, r) => acc + Math.pow(2, -r), 0);
    return (this.m * this.m) / sum;
  }

  hash64(x) { /* MurmurHash3 or similar */ }
  leadingZeros(x) { /* Count leading zeros in x */ }
}
```

## Why This Question Is Asked
Tests the ability to balance space, accuracy, and latency for streaming distinct counts under a sliding window constraint.

## Key Concepts
- Counting Bloom Filter with aging counters
- Sliding window semantics and estimation bias
- Memory-accuracy trade-offs: probabilistic versus exact data structures

## Code Example
```javascript
class SlidingWindowCBF {
  constructor(m, k, windowSize) {
    this.counters = new Array(m).fill(0);
    this.ringBuffer = new Array(windowSize);
    this.head = 0;
    this.k = k;
  }

  hash(id, j) {
    const h = (id * 31 + j * 17) % m;
    return Math.abs(h);
  }

  add(id) {
    for (let j = 0; j < this.k; j++) {
      this.counters[this.hash(id, j)]++;
    }
    this.ringBuffer[this.head] = id;
    this.head = (this.head + 1) % this.ringBuffer.length;
  }

  remove(id) {
    for (let j = 0; j < this.k; j++) {
      this.counters[this.hash(id, j)]--;
    }
  }

  estimateDistinct() {
    return this.counters.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0) / this.k;
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests mastery of amortized batching, range updates, and exact coverage checks on a line, common in streaming analytics and scheduling systems.

## Key Concepts
- Coordinate compression, segment tree with range updates, global min check, amortized batching, space efficiency.

## Code Example
```javascript
class SegTree {
  constructor(n){ this.n=n; this.min=Array(4*n).fill(0); this.cover=Array(4*n).fill(0); this.lazy=Array(4*n).fill(0); }
  _apply(i,delta){ this.cover[i]+=delta; this.min[i]+=delta; this.lazy[i]+=delta; }
  _push(i){ if(this.lazy[i]!==0){ this._apply(i*2,this.lazy[i]); this._apply(i*2+1,this.lazy[i]); this.lazy[i]=0; } }
  update(qL,qR,delta,i=1,l=0,r=this.n-1){ if(qR<l||r<qL) return; if(qL<=l&&r<=qR){ this._apply(i,delta); return; } this._push(i); const m=(l+r)>>1; this.update(qL,qR,delta,i*2,l,m); this.update(qL,qR,delta,i*2+1,m+1,r); this.min[i]=Math.min(this.min[i*2],this.min[i*2+1]); }
  fullCovered(){ return this.min[1]>0; }
}
``` 

